{ "answer": ["renal carcinoma","Renal cancer","kidney cancer","Renal cell carcinoma","RCC","kidney tumor","renal tumor","kidney carcinoma","renal CA"], "clues": ["The first formal classification system for this was proposed in 1826 by König. Before modern microscopic, genetic, and molecular pathology, he divided these into scirrhous, steatomatous, fungoid, and medullary purely based on their appearance.","Because some of these contained lipid-rich cells resembling the adrenocortical zona fasciculata, in 1883, Grawitz theorized that some of these originated from displaced or ectopic adrenal tissue that became trapped in nearby structures.","Roughly 70% of cases are found incidentally on CT or ultrasound as early stages can be asymptomatic. The most common type is named for the clear or pale appearance of the cells under a microscope due to high levels of glycogen and lipids."] },
{ "answer": ["DKA","Diabetic ketoacidosis"], "clues": ["In the 1850s, physicians first began documenting affected patients' breath smelled like 'vinegar, chloroform, rotting apples, or stale beer'. They believed that the patient's internal organs were undergoing spontaneous fermentation, causing patients to become lethally drunk and slip into a coma","A German physician observed that patients did not gasp or choke, but instead took abnormally deep, rhythmic, and sighing breaths. We now know this is an involuntary effort to blow off carbon dioxide to reduce the acidity of the blood","This condition happens when patients cannot move glucose from the blood into cells leading the liver to break down body fat instead. For 50% of toddlers, this is the first manifestation that they have an underlying endocrine disorder"] },
{ "answer": ["abscess","abscesses","purulent SSTI","purulent skin and soft tissue infection"], "clues": ["In 1686, the French King XIV developed this condition. The king's chief surgeon, Charles-François Félix had never performed the delicate procedure before so gathered over 75 poor people, prisoners, and patients in Parisian hospitals who suffered from the same painful condition and operated on them first to perfect his technique.","In ancient Babylon, treating this skin condition was not a minor task, particularly when near critical areas like the eyes and temple. Under the Code of Hammurabi, if a physician used a bronze lancet and the patient died or lost their vision the law could punish the surgeon harshly, including cutting off the surgeon's hands, although this typically only applied if the patient was an aristocrat","The name comes from a Latin word meaning 'a going away' or 'departure' based on the old medical belief that humors or morbid matter would depart from the body through the pus. Most commonly caused by Staphylococcus aureus, incision and drainage is frequently required to treat this."] },
{ "answer": ["Blastomycosis","Blastomyces"], "clues": ["When analyzing the skeletal remains of the Lake Woodland indigenous peoples of North America, who lived roughly between 500 and 1000AD, paleopathologists noticed a stark uptick in destructive bone lesions compared to earlier eras. Researchers hypothesize that as these ancient societies shifted toward intensive agriculture, they inadvertently dug up and inhaled massive amounts of spores from this organism, contributing to shortened lifespans observed during the agricultural transition","Dogs, especially hunting, sporting, and large breeds that spend time sniffing and digging in wet soil, are up to ten times more susceptible to this fungus than humans. It is not typically contagious from animals to humans","In the chilly environment of outdoor dirt and wood, this grows as a harmless, fuzzy mold. However, once its tiny spores are inhaled into the warm human or animal body, they morph into large, multiplying yeasts that can invade tissue. Under the microscope, the yeast can be easily recognized by its unique 'broad-based buds'."] },
{ "answer": ["levodopa","L-DOPA","sinemet"], "clues": ["A natural form of this medication from was the powdered seeds of Mucuna pruriens (velvet bean) used in ancient India around 300BCE. It wasn't until 1911 that it was synthesized in a lab and a Swiss biochemist isolated the pure L-isomer from fava beans. Curious about its effects, he ingested 2.5 grams of it himself and vomited violently. As a result, no one bothered to patent it.","This drug was immortalized in Oliver Sacks's 1973 book and subsequent 1990 movie, Awakenings, starring Robin Williams and Robert De Niro. In 1969, Sacks administered the experimental drug to patients at a New York hospital who had been frozen in a catatonic, statue-like state for decades. While the drug initially awakened them to a normal life, the patients developed severe involuntary movements, hallucinations, and chaotic mood swings","By the mid-1970s, scientists paired this drug with carbidopa, which blocks conversion into dopamine in the bloodstream, allowing it to safely cross the blood-brain barrier. This eliminated the severe associated vomiting giving the combination medication its name meaning 'without emesis'."] },
{ "answer": ["reactive arthritis"], "clues": ["In the 4th century BC, Hippocrates wrote about the two primary pathways that this disease develops although it may have also been described in the Chester Beatty VI medical papyrus from Egypt around 1200BC.","In 1916, German bacteriologist Hans Conrad Julius Reiter described this condition which led to it being widely named Reiter's syndrome. Decades later, it was revealed that Reiter was an early member of the Nazi party and oversaw fatal human experiments on concentration camp prisoners. This led to his name being stripped from the disease.","Modern retrospective medical analyses suggest Columbus suffered from this disease. At the age of 41, he began having episodes of joint pain, fevers, and an aggressive eye inflammation so severe that his eyes frequently bled. Experts believe he may have had HLA-B27 and contracted food poisoning, likely from Shigella or Salmonella, that triggered a severe, lifelong systematic autoimmune response"] },
{ "answer": ["DISH","Diffuse Idiopathic Skeletal Hyperostosis"], "clues": ["This is considered by paleopathologists to be the oldest known rheumatic disorder. Evidence of this has been discovered in dinosaur fossils and early prehistoric mammals","This spinal disorder has been found in several ancient Egyptian mummies, including Pharaoh Merneptah, his father Ramesses II, and Amenhotep III, although it is not strictly hereditary. These mummies were misdiagnosed for years until they went through modern CT scans.","This is a non-inflammatory condition where ligaments and tendons harden and turn into bone. On X-rays, the flowing bone spurs along the side of the spine can look like wax dripping down the side of a candle."] },
{ "answer": ["thalassemia","alpha thalassemia","beta thalassemia"], "clues": ["During the Neolithic Revolution (around 10,000 to 7,000 years ago), humans transitioned from mobile hunter-gatherers to settled farmers, leading to a change in disease exposure. Over millenia, the frequency of the gene causing this disorder rose dramatically, specifically in the Mediterranean basin, the Middle East, the Indian subcontinent, and Southeast Asia","This condition is now diagnosed around the world, brought by the Ottoman Empire expansion, Arab Conquests, and centuries of migration. Because of this, medical systems around the world now routinely screen for this as a standard part of prenatal or neonatal care.","Split into alpha and beta, carrying a singly copy of the gene can offer partial protection against severe malaria."] },
{ "answer": ["scoliosis"], "clues": ["Early treatments for this condition prescribed by Hippocrates included pulling patients on wooden ladders or binding them tightly to sticks","In the early 1800s, French surgeons used mechanical traction to treat this condition which caused so many cases of paralysis that the Royal Academy of Medicine intervened, discredited the methods, and forced the creation of a specialized research commission","The name of this comes from an ancient Greek term meaning 'bend'. Around 90% of affected children do not need surgery and only require routine monitoring or a back brace during growth spurts"] },
{ "answer": ["nasal polyps"], "clues": ["According to medical texts up through the 18th century, the official predisposing triggers for these included indigestion, disorders of the mind, malignant fever, and habitual drunkenness","",""] },
